INT. FIELD HOSPITAL - CONVERTED TRAIN CARRIAGE - NIGHT

A passenger coach gutted for surgery. Cots where seats were. Hand-pumped lamps swing with the rails. A plywood table bolted to the floor.

DR. ISADORA FONTAINE (40s, surgical cap, sleeves rolled) leans over a patient on the table. NURSE KWABENA ASANTE (30s, steady hands) holds retractors. ORDERLY SZYMON (19, sleeves too long) feeds a hand-crank fan. A fourth figure, a SOLDIER in his twenties, lies still under the sheet with his boot half off.

FONTAINE
Everyone, suction. Now.

Kwabena and Szymon both lunge for the pump. They collide, hands tangled on the handle.

KWABENA
Mine.

SZYMON
Sorry. Sorry.

FONTAINE
(to herself)
Come on. Come on, you stubborn thing.

The train lurches on a curve. Instruments slide. Fontaine pins the tray with her forearm. Kwabena catches a clamp mid-air.

KWABENA
Doctor, pressure is dropping.

FONTAINE
I see it. Szymon, more light, left side.

SZYMON
Which left?

FONTAINE
Yours.

He swings the lamp over. The soldier on the table gasps and arches. Everyone stills.

SOLDIER
(hoarse)
Don't cut my leg.

FONTAINE
We're not cutting your leg.

SOLDIER
Promise.

FONTAINE
I promise. What's your name?

SOLDIER
Corporal. Corporal Adeyemi.

FONTAINE
(to the room)
Okay, Adeyemi. We're going to keep you.

ADEYEMI
(whispering, to no one)
Mama, please.

Kwabena and Szymon exchange a look. Szymon wipes his eyes on his sleeve. The fan hums.

FONTAINE
Last stitch. Hold the line. Everybody breathe out.

The carriage exhales. The train rattles over a bridge, and for one second all four of them are still, as if the whole world is balanced on a thread.
